1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,500
Whether you have played Viewfinder or not, the concept of a puzzle game where you can capture a section of the world and place it wherever you need is both intriguing and innovative.

2
00:00:10,040 --> 00:00:14,940
This idea captured my imagination, and here is my attempt to bring it to life in Unity.

3
00:00:22,740 --> 00:00:26,800
Polygon 3D models consist of faces, edges and vertices.

4
00:00:26,800 --> 00:00:33,060
When slicing them in real time, it's crucial to consider the complexity and polycount of the geometry.

5
00:00:33,720 --> 00:00:40,040
Cutting intricate models demands significant computational power, which can lead to serious performance issues.

6
00:00:40,740 --> 00:00:43,760
This was one of the first things I noticed while playing the game.

7
00:00:44,280 --> 00:00:48,380
The level geometries are simple, composed of basic shapes to avoid such problems.

8
00:00:49,380 --> 00:00:53,420
To cut a model, we can use the Plane class along with some calculations.

9
00:00:55,260 --> 00:01:03,540
First, we define a plane, an infinite flat surface that represents the cutting position, splitting the 3D space into two sides.

10
00:01:04,820 --> 00:01:11,560
Next, we determine which vertices of the geometry fall on each side and generate two separate meshes.

11
00:01:12,400 --> 00:01:17,040
During this process, we encounter triangles that have vertices on both sides.

12
00:01:17,560 --> 00:01:22,380
These triangles are key in determining the new vertices needed to create fresh triangles.

13
00:01:22,380 --> 00:01:24,960
Fill in the gaps created by the cut.

14
00:01:29,160 --> 00:01:33,240
Next, we need a method to determine what to slice and what to preserve.

15
00:01:33,920 --> 00:01:38,880
It's clear that slicing affects everything within the camera's view borders.

16
00:01:39,800 --> 00:01:46,380
So we use the camera's frustum shape to create colliders that can scan the environment through the collision system.

17
00:01:47,560 --> 00:01:52,740
To detect objects at the edges, we will generate 4 parallelepipeds.

18
00:01:53,360 --> 00:01:57,960
And for everything within the view, we will use a large trapezoid to cover the entire frustum.

19
00:01:59,180 --> 00:02:02,280
These thin colliders also define the cutting position.

20
00:02:03,100 --> 00:02:05,900
So we use their transform to generate slicing planes.

21
00:02:06,920 --> 00:02:09,160
Following the logic, we previously established.

22
00:02:10,160 --> 00:02:12,540
With that, we have built the core of the system.

23
00:02:15,900 --> 00:02:20,920
Before continuing with the game mechanics, let's first create our environmental level.

24
00:02:21,580 --> 00:02:26,680
Using Blender, we can model simple objects that mimic the game's original aesthetic.

25
00:02:27,440 --> 00:02:32,140
The puzzle is shaped by how we position walls, floors and other elements,

26
00:02:32,560 --> 00:02:35,520
blending in some of the logic pattern from the original game.

27
00:02:36,340 --> 00:02:39,480
We will take a look at the final result at the end.

28
00:02:40,140 --> 00:02:44,140
Let's also create the levels and portal and the camera model.

29
00:02:52,520 --> 00:02:55,120
Big ground elements play a crucial role.

30
00:02:55,680 --> 00:02:59,540
At first glance, they might look like a part of a standard HDRI,

31
00:02:59,940 --> 00:03:03,320
or something mapped onto a sphere with reversed normals.

32
00:03:03,720 --> 00:03:04,760
But that's not the case.

33
00:03:04,760 --> 00:03:10,100
The objects are real, and you can appreciate the parallax effect as you move around.

34
00:03:10,680 --> 00:03:13,660
This is important because you will want to be able to cut them.

35
00:03:14,260 --> 00:03:17,640
In Viewfinder, you expect to slice through everything in the frame,

36
00:03:18,240 --> 00:03:21,300
and that's one of the most satisfying parts of the game.

37
00:03:24,200 --> 00:03:28,720
PS, some elements in the game, like the cat, can be cut or copied.

38
00:03:29,200 --> 00:03:32,880
You can replicate this by applying layer filtering to specific objects.

39
00:03:33,880 --> 00:03:39,220
The developers meticulously design every detail of the world to emphasize the slicing mechanic.

40
00:03:39,860 --> 00:03:45,060
For instance, what appears to be fog below the horizon is actually a large object

41
00:03:45,060 --> 00:03:48,000
with a gradient material simulating a fog effect.

42
00:03:48,940 --> 00:03:53,000
Additionally, all the scenes lighting is baked into the model's textures.

43
00:03:54,420 --> 00:03:58,240
If we rely on real-time lighting, objects spawned from the picture

44
00:03:58,240 --> 00:04:02,360
wouldn't match the original shading since they would interact with light differently.

45
00:04:03,100 --> 00:04:06,480
By baking lights and shadows into the textures in Blender,

46
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:11,180
and using unlit materials, we create a consistent and unchanging environment.

47
00:04:11,960 --> 00:04:16,360
For educational purposes, we will use HDRP fog below the horizon

48
00:04:16,360 --> 00:04:19,600
to demonstrate the limitation it brings to this type of scenario.

49
00:04:20,920 --> 00:04:26,600
Our player character uses a basic character controller that can move, jump, and rotate.

50
00:04:27,600 --> 00:04:31,560
A camera model is parented to the player so it can be carried around.

51
00:04:32,300 --> 00:04:36,340
The camera and picture states are controlled by an Animator controller,

52
00:04:37,080 --> 00:04:40,360
requiring animations like look in and look out,

53
00:04:41,020 --> 00:04:44,920
as well as a shaking animation for when the player is walking or idling.

54
00:04:45,620 --> 00:04:49,420
To create a second point of view, or the camera's eye,

55
00:04:50,280 --> 00:04:54,900
we need a secondary Unity camera set to render on the square ratio render texture.

56
00:04:54,900 --> 00:04:59,600
The key is to disable the second camera at the moment the shot is taken.

57
00:05:00,520 --> 00:05:04,680
So the render texture freezes, capturing a specific moment in time,

58
00:05:05,380 --> 00:05:06,760
just like a real photograph.

59
00:05:07,960 --> 00:05:11,760
Having a second Unity camera unlocks creative possibilities.

60
00:05:12,240 --> 00:05:17,460
For example, you can use it to display objects that are only visible through the camera lens

61
00:05:17,460 --> 00:05:23,400
by filtering with culling masks, or apply different post-processing effects to each camera.

62
00:05:25,220 --> 00:05:29,640
Now, it's time to integrate the player's input with the cutting mechanic.

63
00:05:30,380 --> 00:05:32,460
Let's start with the shooting mechanic.

64
00:05:33,200 --> 00:05:36,900
When a picture is taken, we apply our logic to cut the environment,

65
00:05:37,400 --> 00:05:40,740
preserving what's within the frustum and hiding what's outside.

66
00:05:41,360 --> 00:05:45,900
Before performing the cut, we duplicate the game objects so that they appear untouched.

67
00:05:46,760 --> 00:05:52,080
The cut objects are then parented to a transform representing the camera's point of view,

68
00:05:52,080 --> 00:05:55,080
allowing them to move and rotate with the player.

69
00:05:55,920 --> 00:05:58,320
You have probably guessed the next step.

70
00:05:58,760 --> 00:06:02,180
To reintegrate the frame view into the world, we cut the environment,

71
00:06:02,600 --> 00:06:07,340
delete the objects inside the frustum, and re-enabled the previously cut objects.

72
00:06:08,180 --> 00:06:09,120
And that's the trick.

73
00:06:10,160 --> 00:06:15,240
Just like in the game, we will also add a button to rotate the picture and hit the objects,

74
00:06:15,780 --> 00:06:18,480
which will open many new puzzle possibilities.

75
00:06:19,300 --> 00:06:23,620
As you can imagine, there is a computational cost during these frames,

76
00:06:24,040 --> 00:06:26,620
which is why it's essential to keep the scene lightweight

77
00:06:26,620 --> 00:06:29,120
and design everything around the core feature.

78
00:06:29,560 --> 00:06:31,580
Of course, this is just a case study.

79
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:35,980
The team behind the game has likely optimized the process significantly

80
00:06:35,980 --> 00:06:38,480
or approach the mechanic differently.

81
00:06:38,920 --> 00:06:42,220
Do you have any other ideas on how to achieve this?

82
00:06:42,640 --> 00:06:43,620
Let me know in the comments.

83
00:06:44,400 --> 00:06:48,340
Also, for more insights into the cutting process and idea,

84
00:06:49,020 --> 00:06:51,780
I recommend checking out this video from Christian League.

85
00:06:52,500 --> 00:06:58,080
Before we reveal the final result, we want to remind you that this is Unity Unite Week.

86
00:06:58,900 --> 00:07:03,160
We will be attending the event in Barcelona as Unity Insiders.

87
00:07:03,920 --> 00:07:08,360
And look forward to connecting with you and exploring Unity's future together.

88
00:07:12,240 --> 00:07:15,160
Now, here is the final result and gameplay.

89
